  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to use a novel time-product optimization approach that leverages continuous optimization with adaptive grid resolution and a sophisticated projection scheme. Unlike previous combinatorial methods, this approach starts with a smooth function approximation and refines it through projected gradient steps, using adaptive grid resolution. This method addresses the non-convex optimization challenge by effectively navigating the solution space through combination of continuous optimization and careful projection, simultaneously improving both solution quality and convergence speed. The limitation is that it requires careful computational resource allocation as grid resolution increases, but the adaptivity allows focusing resources where improvements are most impactful.\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Representation: A step function h(x) defined on [0,2] with integral constraint \u222bh(x)dx=1 (over [0,2]).\n- Variables: Continuous optimization space projected periodically to the discrete grid representation.\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1\u2212h(x+k))dx, approximated via grid convolution and scaling by dx.\n- Constraints:\n  - 0 \u2264 h\u1d62 \u2264 1 \u2200i\n  - \u2211h\u1d62 * dx = 1\n- Projection: Grid-based realization of the continuous function using linear interpolation between grid points.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialize grid (n=80, then adaptively increase to 320)\n2. Start with parent initialization if available\n3. Perform continuous optimization with the following steps:\n   - Gradient computation via central differences\n   - Projective descent along coordinate-wise updates\n   - Progressive grid refinement based on non-improvement thresholds\n   - Adaptive time budget allocation based on gradient consistency\n4. After optimization performs endpoint analysis and final descent\n5. Output best solution with score recomputation\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Base grid: 80 (initial), targets 160, 320\n- Time budget allocation: 80% to optimization, 20% to final refinement\n- Coarse-to-fine strategy with dynamic resolution\n- Restart mechanism for stuck solutions\n\nComputebudgetandrobustness:\n- Base configuration handles up to 320 grid points within 1100 seconds\n- Progresses 8 batch jobs per grid resolution\n- Fails only when local minima are detected and no escaping strategy works\n\nValidationandreturncontract:\n- Every solution must: 0\u2264h\u1d62\u22641, \u2211h\u1d62*dx\u22481\n- Objective recomputed via the standard programmatic evaluator\n- Input dimensions consistent with objective specification",
